1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,697
In hardware in the loop, the robot's real computer,

2
00:00:03,697 --> 00:00:06,331
an NVIDIA Jetson, runs the robot software,

3
00:00:06,331 --> 00:00:10,844
while Isaac Sim on your workstation simulates the world and the sensors.

4
00:00:11,744 --> 00:00:15,456
Isaac Sim publishes clock, scan and camera topics.

5
00:00:15,456 --> 00:00:20,207
They travel over the local network as DDS traffic to the Jetson,

6
00:00:20,207 --> 00:00:24,662
where Isaac ROS and your perception or navigation nodes run.

7
00:00:24,662 --> 00:00:27,705
Velocity commands come back the same way.

8
00:00:28,605 --> 00:00:31,489
Four things to check on the simulation side.

9
00:00:31,489 --> 00:00:36,339
Both machines on the same network, with the same domain id and middleware.

10
00:00:36,339 --> 00:00:39,616
Publish the simulation clock with the clock graph.

11
00:00:39,616 --> 00:00:43,089
Set use sim time to true for the nodes on the Jetson.

12
00:00:43,089 --> 00:00:47,939
And if the Jetson runs Humble, launch Isaac Sim with the Humble libraries.

13
00:00:47,939 --> 00:00:51,806
The Jetson setup itself follows the course pages unchanged.

14
00:00:51,806 --> 00:00:53,510
That completes the course.

15
00:00:53,510 --> 00:00:58,098
Congratulations, and enjoy building robots in Isaac Sim six point one.

